# deploy/herald-translate-check.service
#
# systemd --user unit for one `pnpm translate:check --notify` — the weekly glossary digest. It asks
# the two questions src/cli/translate-check.ts's own doc comment names: did our draft use the decided
# term the source called for (`checkGlossary`), and which decided terms do the humans keep dropping
# once a post actually goes out (`checkPublishedOverrides`). Read-only from start to finish.
#
# Why it needs a schedule at all: the drift it catches is invisible one item at a time — `narrative`
# rendered 이야기 is a perfectly good sentence, wrong only against a decision recorded elsewhere.
# Measured on 2026-08-07, a batch of nineteen translations had drifted from four decided terms and
# every individual sentence read fine. The command has existed since then and gets run when somebody
# remembers, which for a report whose entire value is catching slow drift is close to not at all.
#
# WHAT THIS UNIT NEVER DOES: change anything. `translate:check` writes nothing, changes no status,
# and never exits non-zero on a finding — a glossary has real exceptions, so this is a list a human
# reads before 1차 검수, not a gate. That has a useful consequence for the hook below: a non-zero exit
# from this unit can only ever mean the run itself broke (no database, no glossary, timeout), never
# that the report had something in it. The alert is therefore never a weekly false alarm, and the
# findings reach a human the other way — `--notify` pages the ops room about question 2's overrides
# (`overrideNotification`, src/cli/translateCheckReport.ts), while question 1's drift deliberately
# never pages and is read out of the run log this unit's wrapper keeps.
#
# NOT installed by any committed script. Copying this into ~/.config/systemd/user/ and running
# `systemctl --user daemon-reload` is a human-supervised step (see the team runbook), same as the
# four units already in this directory.
#
# Machine-specific by design, not templated: node and pnpm are reached through `%h/.herald/bin` on
# the one machine this runs on — see the PATH= comment below, and herald-watch.service's longer
# version of it.

[Unit]
Description=Mantle KR Herald glossary drift digest — one weekly `pnpm translate:check --notify`
# The same templated hook the other four scheduled units use. `%n` expands to this unit's own name
# before systemd resolves the target, so the alert names this unit and tails this unit's journal, not
# another's — the `OnFailure=bar@%n.service` idiom `man systemd.unit` documents, and the reason the
# hook became a template at all (see deploy/herald-notify-failure@.service's header).
#
# Worth more here than the once-a-day units it copies, not less, and for a reason specific to what
# `--notify` sends: `overrideNotification` returns undefined when there are no overrides, so a
# healthy week with a clean glossary sends NOTHING. Silence is therefore the normal successful
# outcome, which means silence cannot also be how a dead fire announces itself. This hook is the only
# thing that separates the two, for seven days at a time.
OnFailure=herald-notify-failure@%n.service

[Service]
Type=oneshot
# The deploy checkout — merged `main` only. Same reasoning as every other unit: whatever is checked
# out here IS what runs at the next fire, and on 2026-08-07 a unit pointed at the development
# checkout ran an unmerged branch's query against production. tests/deploy/workingDirectory.test.ts
# fails if this ever names a development checkout.
#
# It decides more than the code for THIS command. `translation/glossary.json` is resolved relative to
# the repo root (`paths.translationConfigDir`, src/paths.ts), so this directory is also where the
# glossary the report is measured against comes from — the copy `pnpm deploy:freeze` lands here from
# the development tree's git-ignored `translation/`, not whatever anyone edited since. A glossary
# edit therefore reaches this report at the next deploy, not the next fire, which is the same
# deliberate delay every other steering value has had since 2026-08-09.
WorkingDirectory=%h/.herald/app

# node and pnpm are reached through %h/.herald/bin, a directory of symlinks, NOT an nvm version
# directory — see herald-watch.service's own PATH comment for the 2026-08-09 rebuild that made this
# necessary. node must be reachable even though pnpm is named by absolute path below: pnpm's own file
# starts `#!/usr/bin/env node`, and `env` resolves that through this PATH. `claude` is deliberately
# not on this path: this command spawns no agent, exactly like herald-creds.service and
# herald-x-reconcile.service and unlike the two units that do.
Environment=PATH=%h/.herald/bin:/home/kyle/.local/bin:/usr/local/sbin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/sbin:/usr/bin:/sbin:/bin

# No HERALD_OUTPUT_DIR here, and it is not an oversight — it was checked against what the command
# actually reads before being left out, the same way herald-x-reconcile.service argues its own
# omission and herald-convert.service argues the three variables it drops.
#
# `translate:check` reads exactly two things. The translations come from `createStores(db)
# .translationStore`, which is `PgTranslationStore` — Postgres, not OUTPUT_DIR's
# `translations/translations.json`, which stopped being the ledger when the eleven stores moved (see
# src/cli/stores.ts's own module comment). The glossary comes from `new JsonGlossaryStore(paths
# .translationConfigDir)`, and that path is `join(REPO_ROOT, "translation")` — repo-root-relative, so
# HERALD_OUTPUT_DIR would not redirect it even if someone set it hoping to. Nothing this command
# touches is OUTPUT_DIR-relative, so setting the variable would only imply an artifact tree this run
# has no relationship with.
#
# No HERALD_TRANSLATE_SINCE, HERALD_WATCH_BATCH, HERALD_CONVERT_BATCH or HERALD_COLLECT_MAX_PAGES
# either. All four bound a `--limit` or a page count belonging to translate:prepare/translate:align,
# convert:prepare or collect, and this command runs none of them — it selects with its own
# `--status`/`--since` flags, which this unit deliberately does not pass (see ExecStart= below).

# Exactly two lines — DATABASE_URL and HERALD_DB_ENV=production — chmod 600, created by Kyle by hand
# (a production connection string does not pass through an agent session). This unit needs it for the
# reason a read-only command needs it most: without it the fire succeeds, exits 0, and reports
# cheerfully on the local Docker database's handful of rows instead of production's — a green weekly
# digest about the wrong data, and the only sign is the database name on the command's first line,
# which nobody reads on a run that found nothing. Everything else comes from the repo's own .env via
# `pnpm translate:check`'s `tsx --env-file-if-exists=.env`, including the TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN and
# TELEGRAM_CHAT_ID_OPS that `--notify` needs; a shell-exported variable wins over Node's --env-file
# (verified on this machine), so these two override .env's local-Docker DATABASE_URL without this
# unit ever touching .env itself.
EnvironmentFile=%h/.herald/prod.env

# Outer bound on a wedged run, and unusually for this directory the number is set by the ALERT path
# rather than by the work. Two parts:
#
#   120s — the database half. One `translationStore.loadAll()` plus connect and close; the checks
#          themselves are in-memory string work over rows already fetched, and the glossary is a
#          local file read. What this has to absorb is a cold Neon compute: at this timer's Monday
#          06:53 slot the last thing to touch the database was the 06:41 reconcile, and Neon
#          autosuspends after about five minutes, so essentially every fire pays a wake-up.
#          herald-convert.service budgets 240s for three round trips including that wake; this is one
#          query, so 120s is the same generosity at a third of the traffic.
#
#   300s — the Telegram half, which is the part that actually sizes this directive. `notifyOps`
#          (src/shared/notifyOps.ts) calls global `fetch` with no timeout of its own, so a Telegram
#          endpoint that accepts the connection and then never answers is bounded only by undici's
#          300s headers timeout. Reaching that timeout is the GOOD outcome: notifyOps never throws —
#          it logs `[notifyOps] failed to notify` and returns — so the run still exits 0 and the
#          report is still in the run log. A TimeoutStartSec below 300 would instead SIGTERM the
#          process mid-send, which turns a working check into a failed unit and fires the OnFailure=
#          hook to say the glossary check failed, when the check had already finished and printed —
#          a false alarm about the one subsystem (Telegram) that is demonstrably down and therefore
#          least likely to deliver the alarm anyway.
#
# 120 + 300 = 420. The retry inside notifyOps does not double the 300: a second attempt only happens
# when the first got a real response back, and a response means it did not hang. The one shape that
# overruns this is Telegram answering slowly AND then hanging the plain-text retry, and at that point
# being killed is the right answer.
#
# The upper bound the rest of this directory argues for — timeout at or under half the fire period,
# or one wedged run holds the unit active across the next OnCalendar= and systemd skips it — is not
# close to binding here: 420s against a 604800s week is 1/1440th of the period.
TimeoutStartSec=420

# Wrapped in herald-run-logged.sh for the reason every other unit is, and more so than any of them:
# journald on this box holds roughly eight minutes of history (it rotates on every backwards clock
# step, and this machine's WSL2 host sync and timesyncd both step the clock constantly), so a report
# printed at 06:53 on Monday is unreadable by 07:05 and the next one is seven days away. This is also
# the only unit here whose SUCCESSFUL output is the entire point — the Telegram notice is a summary,
# and `%h/.herald/logs/herald-translate-check/` is where the full grouped list of drifts and
# overrides actually lives when someone wants to read them item by item.
#
# Argument order is that wrapper's contract, not a convention: `$1` is the unit name and everything
# after it is the command, run verbatim. Reversed, the wrapper names its log directory after pnpm and
# tries to run the unit name as a command (measured: exit 127). `%n` also keys the per-unit log
# directory, the same key deploy/herald-notify-failure.sh derives its fallback excerpt from, so the
# two agree without either hardcoding a unit list. tests/deploy/runLogging.test.ts holds the property
# that matters most — the command's exit status reaches systemd unchanged, since a wrapper that
# swallowed it would switch this unit's alert off permanently and invisibly.
#
# `--notify` is not optional decoration, and it is spelled out here for the reason
# herald-x-reconcile.service spells out `--yes`: it is off by default (an interactive run stays
# stdout-only), so without it every fire writes a report into a log nobody is tailing and exits 0 — a
# scheduler that looks armed and delivers nothing. It sends one grouped ops message for the
# published-override half and nothing at all when there are none, so the flag costs nothing on a
# clean week and is the only reason a dirty one reaches anybody.
#
# No `--status`, no `--since`, no `--published`. `--status`/`--since` narrow the row set, and the
# standing whole-ledger view is exactly what a weekly digest is for — a narrowed one would answer
# "did last week drift?" while the question this report exists for is "what has drifted?".
# `--published` is not passed either: it would restrict question 1 to rows that already carry a
# published text and skip the rest, and the published-override half of the report (question 2) runs
# unconditionally anyway, so it would cost coverage and buy nothing. Anyone who wants either view
# runs the command by hand — this unit's job is the standing one.
#
# Ordering note for whoever installs this: the wrapper lives in the deploy checkout, so
# `bash deploy/herald-deploy.sh` must have run (landing this commit in %h/.herald/app) BEFORE this
# unit file is copied into ~/.config/systemd/user/.
ExecStart=%h/.herald/app/deploy/herald-run-logged.sh %n %h/.herald/bin/pnpm translate:check --notify
